! =============================================================================
! SaltFoss Training — Segment 3 (0:16-0:28): The chemical potential trap (2/3)
! =============================================================================
! Remedy #1: perturb one mass slightly off the exact stoichiometric ratio.
! This breaks the degeneracy and gives element potentials a well-defined
! (if numerically "arbitrary") value. Compare mu_F, mu_Be, mu_Li below against
! 02a_underdetermined.ti -- individual potentials swing by 20-40 kJ/mol -- but
! then compute mu(LiF)=mu_Li+mu_F and mu(BeF2)=mu_Be+2*mu_F and see those are
! INVARIANT to <0.1 kJ/mol. Only the species-level combination is physical.
!
! Run:    ./bin/InputScriptMode inputs/training/saltfoss/02b_perturbed.ti
! Verified output (kJ/mol): mu_F=-97.9  mu_Be=-927.1  mu_Li=-574.2
!   -> mu(LiF) = -672.10    mu(BeF2) = -1122.95   (matches 02a to <0.1 kJ/mol)
! Integral Gibbs energy is IDENTICAL to 02a: -2.46715E+06 J.
! =============================================================================
temperature       = 900
pressure          = 1.0
mass(3)           = 2.0        ! Li
mass(4)           = 1.0        ! Be
mass(9)           = 4.00001    ! F  -- perturbed by 1 part in 400,000
temperature unit  = K
pressure unit     = atm
mass unit         = moles
data file         = data/MSD-TC_V4.1_Fluorides_No_Func.dat
print mode        = 2
